'''Champions of the Future''' ('''COTF''') is a [[kart racing]] competition organised by the RGMMC Group and sanctioned by [[FIA Karting]] ({{nowrap|CIK-FIA}}). Founded in 2020, COTF is one of 11 competitions on the [[List of kart racing championships#Active international championships|international kart racing calendar]] in [[Commission Internationale de Karting#Categories|{{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} classes]].

Initially a two-round series in [[KF2|OK]] and {{nowrap|[[OK-Junior]]}} ({{nowrap|OK-J}})—with single-round finals in [[KZ2]] and [[60 Mini]]—the championship has since expanded to all eight {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} classes across the: '''Euro Series''' ([[KF2|OK]], {{nowrap|[[KF3|OK-J]]}}), '''Shifters''' ([[KZ (karting)|KZ]], [[KZ2]], {{nowrap|[[KZ2-M]]}}), and '''Academy Program''' ({{nowrap|[[OK-N]]}}, {{nowrap|[[OKN-J]]}}, [[60 Mini]]), the latter supported by [[F1 Academy]]. COTF also hosted the one-off '''Winter Series''' at [[Autodromo di Franciacorta|Franciacorta]] in 2022. COTF is set to expand to national series in 2026, hosting a British title with [[Motorsport UK]], an [[United Arab Emirates|Emirati]] title, a [[Macau|Macanese]] title, and an [[United States|American]] title.

The Euro Series and Shifters events traditionally take place in the two weeks prior to a [[Karting World Championship|World]] or [[Karting European Championship|European Championship]] round at the same [[kart circuit|circuit]]. Notable former champions include [[Nikita Bedrin]] (2020, OK), [[Arvid Lindblad]] (2020, {{nowrap|OK-J}}), [[Rafael Câmara]] (2021, OK), [[Freddie Slater (racing driver)|Freddie Slater]] (2021, {{nowrap|OK-J}}), [[Alex Powell]] (2023, OK), and [[Dries Van Langendonck]] (2024, {{nowrap|OK-J}}). [[Luna Fluxá]] (2024, {{nowrap|OK-N}}) is the only [[List of female racing drivers|female driver]] to win a title.

== History ==
=== Background (1962–2019) ===
[[File:KZ PREGRID KSP 6 7810-edit-660x330.jpg|thumb|left|The RGMMC Group has been the official [[promoter (entertainment)|promoter]] of {{nowrap|[[CIK-FIA]]}} events—the [[Karting World Championship|World]] and [[Karting European Championship|European]] Championships—since 2018.|alt=The pre-grid of the 2015 KZ World Championship at Le Mans]]
The [[Commission Internationale de Karting]] ({{nowrap|CIK-FIA}}) was founded in 1962 by the [[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] (FIA) to govern the rising international sport of [[kart racing]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/11030/pre-birth-cik-some-50-years-ago |title=Pre-birth of the CIK some 50 years ago |date=12 March 2012 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=20 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240723043207/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/11030/pre-birth-cik-some-50-years-ago |archive-date=23 July 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> The inaugural [[CIK-FIA World Championship]] was hosted two years later at the ''[[Pista d'Oro]]'' in [[Rome]], which again held the event in 1965.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://tkart.it/en/news/features/lalbo-doro-del-campionato-del-mondo-di-karting |title=L'Albo d'Oro del Campionato del Mondo di Karting |last=Mele |first=Giovanni |date=14 September 2019 |website=TKART |access-date=20 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250512220055/https://tkart.it/en/news/features/lalbo-doro-del-campionato-del-mondo-di-karting |archive-date=12 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} began its [[homologation (motorsport)|homologation]] of distinct classes in the discipline in 1974, when the first 125&nbsp;cc [[gearbox]] category was created for the [[Karting European Championship|European Championship]]: [[KZ (karting)|Formula C]] (FC).<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://issuu.com/vroomkartinternational/docs/vroom_int._175_lr |title=Special – CIK-FIA Hall of Fame 1964–2015 |date=January 2016 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=18 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |pages=28–43 |edition=175 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240918191252/https://issuu.com/vroomkartinternational/docs/vroom_int._175_lr |archive-date=18 September 2024 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The 100&nbsp;cc [[direct-drive]] category was clarified in 1976, when [[KF1|Formula Europe]] was introduced to control technical evolutions and costs.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fiakarting.com/history/1976 |title=1976 {{!}} History |website=[[FIA Karting]] |publisher=[[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] |access-date=20 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250614061556/https://www.fiakarting.com/history/1976 |archive-date=14 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The direct-drive classes were adapted in 1981, when the 135&nbsp;cc [[Formula K]] (FK), 100&nbsp;cc [[Intercontinental A]] (ICA), and [[Junior Intercontinental A]] ({{nowrap|ICA-J}}) were introduced to the World Championship, European Championship, and [[Karting World Cup|World Cup]], respectively.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fiakarting.com/history/1981 |title=1981 {{!}} History |website=[[FIA Karting]] |publisher=[[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] |access-date=20 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250614061556/https://www.fiakarting.com/history/1981 |archive-date=14 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

By 2020, there were five [[Commission Internationale de Karting#Categories|{{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} classes]]: [[KF2|OK]], the primary senior direct-drive class; {{nowrap|[[OK-Junior]]}} ({{nowrap|OK-J}}), the under-15 direct-drive class; [[KZ (karting)|KZ]], the primary gearbox class; [[KZ2]], the secondary gearbox class; and [[60 Mini]], the under-12 international class.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://backend.fiakarting.com/sites/default/files/FIA_KARTING_PRESENTATION_DOSSIER_2020_vEN_2.pdf |title=2020 Season {{!}} Presentation Dossier |date=28 January 2020 |website=[[FIA Karting]] |publisher=[[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217012545/https://backend.fiakarting.com/sites/default/files/FIA_KARTING_PRESENTATION_DOSSIER_2020_vEN_2.pdf |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fiakarting.com/news/mini-now-has-international-technical-regulations |title=The Mini now has international technical regulations |date=29 January 2020 |website=[[FIA Karting]] |publisher=[[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217012849/https://www.fiakarting.com/news/mini-now-has-international-technical-regulations |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Rotax]] was founded in 1920 as an [[internal combustion engine]] manufacturer and entered the FK class in 1983,<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/38883/the-history-of-karting-at-rotax |title=The History of Karting at Rotax |last=Giuliani |first=Giuliano Ciucci |date=17 March 2021 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=17 December 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217015301/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/38883/the-history-of-karting-at-rotax |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> later founding the [[Rotax Max Challenge]] in 2000.<ref>{{Cite magazine |title=Rotax Max Challenge Grand Finals {{!}} A beautiful story |last=Giuliani |first=Giuliano Ciucci |date=15 December 2021 |magazine=Vroomkart |issn=1724-9147 |page=2}}</ref> [[Italian American Motor Engineering]] (IAME) was founded as a [[kart manufacturer]] in 1968 as a [[Mergers and acquisitions|merger]] between [[Parilla (motorcycle manufacturer)|Parilla]] and [[Komet Racing Tyres|Komet]]; it began operating its [[one-design racing|single-design]] championships, the [[IAME Series]], in 2007.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://tkart.it/en/magazine/editorial/began-story-of-iame |url-access=subscription |title=Bruno Grana and IAME {{!}} How it all began |date=12 May 2019 |website=TKART |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217012932/https://tkart.it/en/magazine/editorial/began-story-of-iame |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fia.com/news/iame-45-years-passion-and-success |title=IAME, 45 years of passion and success |date=5 February 2014 |website=[[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217013151/https://www.fia.com/news/iame-45-years-passion-and-success |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://tkart.it/en/magazine/under-review/x30-125-cc-iame-series-single-brand-championship-kart-engine-setup-maintenance-bench-testing |title=X30 125 cc, the "premier" kart engine in the IAME Series single-brand championship |last=Mantovani |first=Giacomo |date=22 March 2023 |website=TKART |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217013228/https://tkart.it/en/magazine/under-review/x30-125-cc-iame-series-single-brand-championship-kart-engine-setup-maintenance-bench-testing#2 |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.ekartingnews.com/2015/10/14/iame-international-final-set-for-this-weekend-at-le-mans/ |title=IAME International Final Set for This Weekend at Le Mans |date=14 October 2015 |website=eKartingNews |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217013426/https://www.ekartingnews.com/2015/10/14/iame-international-final-set-for-this-weekend-at-le-mans/ |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Switzerland-based RGMMC Group had been established in 2004, later becoming the [[Promoter (entertainment)|promoter]] of Rotax events in 2006, IAME events in 2016, and {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} events in 2018.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.motorsport-xl.de/news/2017/CIK-FIA/Offiziell-RGMMC-Group-neuer-CIK-FIA-Promotor-ab-2018-29547.html |title=Offiziell: RGMMC Group neuer CIK-FIA-Promotor ab 2018 |trans-title=Official: RGMMC Group new CIK-FIA promoter from 2018 |date=29 November 2017 |website=Motorsport XL |language=de |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217013522/https://www.motorsport-xl.de/news/2017/CIK-FIA/Offiziell-RGMMC-Group-neuer-CIK-FIA-Promotor-ab-2018-29547.html |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Across 2018 and 2019 with the latter, RGMMC promoted six World Championships, two World Cups, and eight European Championships.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/31478/cik-fia-released-the-new-2018-calendar |title=CIK-FIA released the new 2018 calendar |date=22 September 2017 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=17 December 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217014038/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/31478/cik-fia-released-the-new-2018-calendar |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://tkart.it/en/news/features/fia-karting-official-calendar |title=FIA Karting – Official Calendar 2019 |last=Bernardi |first=Max |date=5 December 2018 |website=TKART |access-date=17 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251217014015/https://tkart.it/en/news/features/fia-karting-official-calendar |archive-date=17 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Championship beginnings (2020–2023) ===
==== 2020: Debut during COVID-19 pandemic ====
[[File:FIA F2 Austria 2025 Nr. 4 Lindblad.jpg|thumb|right|[[Arvid Lindblad]] (pictured in [[2025 Spielberg Formula 2 round|2025]]) won the inaugural {{nowrap|[[OK-J]]}} title in 2020.|alt=Arvid Lindblad driving the Dallara F2-2025 at the 2025 Spielberg Formula 2 round]]
COTF was founded in 2020 by the RGMMC Group to promote and develop four {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} classes—OK, {{nowrap|OK-J}}, KZ2, and 60 Mini.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartxpress.com/ReadMore/rgmmc-reveals-new-calendar-champions-of-the-future |title=RGMMC reveals new calendar Champions of the Future |last=Kaligis |first=Bas |date=21 April 2020 |website=KartXpress |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210123095403/https://www.kartxpress.com/ReadMore/rgmmc-reveals-new-calendar-champions-of-the-future |archive-date=23 January 2021 |url-status=live}}</ref> Its inaugural round at [[ProKart Raceland|Wackersdorf]] on 7–10 May was delayed and cancelled amidst the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Europe]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://theracebox.com/rgmmc-champions-of-the-future-calendar-revised/ |title=RGMMC Champions of the Future calendar revised |last=Alvarez |first=Guillaume |date=20 April 2020 |website=The RaceBox |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230607080229/https://theracebox.com/rgmmc-champions-of-the-future-calendar-revised/ |archive-date=7 June 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> as was the [[Åsum Ring|Kristianstad]] round on 25–28 June,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2020/05/19/champions-of-the-future-kristianstad-cancellation/ |title=Champions of the Future: Kristianstad cancellation |date=19 May 2020 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221204022645/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2020/05/19/champions-of-the-future-kristianstad-cancellation/ |archive-date=4 December 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> leaving [[Circuito Internacional de Zuera|Zuera]] as the debut [[kart circuit|circuit]] of the series on 16–19 July.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/37361/international-season-begins-in-zuera |title=International season begins in Zuera |last=Giuliani |first=Giuliano Ciucci |date=8 June 2020 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240417040424/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/37361/international-season-begins-in-zuera |archive-date=17 April 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> Over 170 drivers entered in preparation for the opening round of the European Championship that month.<ref name="2020KZ260M">{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2020/07/20/high-driver-attendance-and-nail-biting-racing-for-the-start-of-champions-of-the-future-by-rgmmc/ |title=High driver attendance and nail-biting racing for the start of Champions of the Future by RGMMC |date=20 July 2020 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220907024813/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2020/07/20/high-driver-attendance-and-nail-biting-racing-for-the-start-of-champions-of-the-future-by-rgmmc/ |archive-date=7 September 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> The second and final round was held on 1 November at [[Algarve International Circuit|Portimão]] in the OK and {{nowrap|OK-J}} classes, serving as direct preparation for the World Championship one week later.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2020/10/21/champions-of-the-future-175-entered-in-portimao/ |title=Champions of the Future: 175 entered in Portimao |date=21 October 2020 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220907024842/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2020/10/21/champions-of-the-future-175-entered-in-portimao/ |archive-date=7 September 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> The inaugural champions were [[Nikita Bedrin]] (OK), [[Arvid Lindblad]] ({{nowrap|OK-J}}), [[Paolo Ippolito]] (KZ2), and [[Dmitry Matveev]] (60 Mini).<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://theracebox.com/first-champions-of-the-future-crowned-at-portimao/ |title=First Champions of the Future crowned at Portimao |last=Alvarez |first=Guillaume |date=3 November 2020 |website=The RaceBox |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250324055116/https://theracebox.com/first-champions-of-the-future-crowned-at-portimao/ |archive-date=24 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="2020KZ260M"/>

==== 2021–2022: Introduction of Euro Series and Academy Program ====
[[File:FRECA 2025 Mogyoród Slater.jpg|thumb|left|World Champion [[Freddie Slater (racing driver)|Freddie Slater]] (pictured in [[2025 Formula Regional European Championship|2025]]) claimed the {{nowrap|OK-J}} title in 2021.|alt=Close-up of Freddie Slater in the Tatuus FR-19 at the 2025 Budapest Formula Regional round]]
The 2021 season was contested in OK and {{nowrap|OK-J}} across three rounds at [[Karting Genk|Genk]], Zuera, and [[Karting Campillos|Campillos]].<ref name="VK2021">{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/39702/champions-of-the-future-closes-the-season-in-campillos |title=Champions Of The Future closes the season in Campillos |last=Corradengo |first=Simone |date=24 September 2021 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240422125542/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/39702/champions-of-the-future-closes-the-season-in-campillos |archive-date=22 April 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> The latter round replaced the [[Autodromo di Franciacorta|Franciacorta]] and [[Circuito Internazionale 7 Laghi Kart|Castelletto]] rounds—which were cancelled due to logistical challenges—and provided support for the upcoming World Championship.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/38700/champions-of-the-future-2021-calendar-approved |title=Champions Of The Future – 2021 calendar approved |last=Corradengo |first=Simone |date=5 February 2021 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240422123118/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/38700/champions-of-the-future-2021-calendar-approved |archive-date=22 April 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://theracebox.com/champions-of-the-futures-2021-calendar-is-complete/ |title=Champions of the Future: Spain & Italy added to 2021 calendar |last=Alvarez |first=Guillaume |date=13 May 2021 |website=The RaceBox |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250324073624/https://theracebox.com/champions-of-the-futures-2021-calendar-is-complete/ |archive-date=24 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="VK2021"/> [[Rafael Câmara]] and reigning World Champion [[Freddie Slater (racing driver)|Freddie Slater]] claimed the championships in OK and {{nowrap|OK-J}}, respectively.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/39124/champions-of-the-future-genk-camara-and-slater-the-winners |title=Champions of The Future, Genk – Câmara and Slater the winners |last=Corradengo |first=Simone |date=2 May 2021 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240613070752/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/39124/champions-of-the-future-genk-camara-and-slater-the-winners |archive-date=13 June 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> Prior to the 2022 season, the one-off '''Champions of the Future Winter Series''' was held at Franciacorta in: OK, won by [[Tomass Štolcermanis]]; {{nowrap|OK-J}}, won by [[Maciej Gładysz]]; and KZ2, won by [[Simo Puhakka]].<ref name="WS2022">{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2022/02/21/winter-series-nearly-140-entries-at-franciacorta/ |title=Winter Series: nearly 140 entries at Franciacorta |date=21 February 2022 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220907013353/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2022/02/21/winter-series-nearly-140-entries-at-franciacorta/ |archive-date=7 September 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/exceptional-inaugural-winter-series-at-franciacorta/ |title=Exceptional Inaugural Winter Series at Franciacorta! |date=18 March 2022 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024816/https://www.championskarting.com/exceptional-inaugural-winter-series-at-franciacorta/ |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The regular season was re-branded as the '''Champions of the Future Euro Series'''—held over four rounds timetabled by [[factory-backed]] teams at Portimão, Zuera, Kristianstad, and Franciacorta—and won by British drivers [[Joe Turney]] (OK) and [[Nathan Tye]] ({{nowrap|OK-J}}).<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2022/01/12/leading-factory-teams-draw-up-a-common-timetable-for-2022/ |title=Leading factory teams draw up a common timetable for 2022 |date=12 January 2022 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220907013915/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2022/01/12/leading-factory-teams-draw-up-a-common-timetable-for-2022/ |archive-date=7 September 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/exciting-finale-in-franciacorta/ |title=Exciting finale in Franciacorta |date=27 June 2022 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024820/https://www.championskarting.com/exciting-finale-in-franciacorta/ |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:F4 Italy 2024 Mugello Alex Powell.jpg|thumb|right|[[Alex Powell]] (pictured in [[2024 Italian F4 Championship|2024]]) won the [[KF2|OK]] Euro Series in 2023.|alt=Alex Powell behind the wheel of the Tatuus F4-T421 in the 2024 Italian F4 Championship at Mugello]]
In 2023, COTF founded the '''Champions of the Future Academy Program''' ('''COTFA''') in collaboration with [[F1 Academy]], a single-round event at [[Al Forsan International Sports Resort|Al Forsan]]—the series' first expansion beyond [[continental Europe]]—in the recently established low-cost {{nowrap|[[OK-National]]}} ({{nowrap|OK-N}}) and {{nowrap|[[OKN-Junior]]}} ({{nowrap|OKN-J}}) classes, as well as 60 Mini.<ref name="AP2023">{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2023/12/02/champions-of-the-future-academy-program-launches-with-long-list-of-innovations-and-achievements/ |title=Champions of the Future Academy Program launches with long list of innovations and achievements! |date=2 December 2023 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240830225141/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2023/12/02/champions-of-the-future-academy-program-launches-with-long-list-of-innovations-and-achievements/ |archive-date=30 August 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/F1-Academy/news/f1-academy-expands-into-karting-with-new-global-series/10532423/ |title=F1 Academy expands into karting with new global series |last=Cleeren |first=Filip |date=13 October 2023 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240803171145/https://www.autosport.com/F1-Academy/news/f1-academy-expands-into-karting-with-new-global-series/10532423/ |archive-date=3 August 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> F1 Academy sponsored the entries of three [[List of female racing drivers|female drivers]] in each class, which Fred Smith of ''[[Road & Track]]'' opined "could completely alter the access women have to [[Formula One]]" within a decade.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/news/a45531451/f1-academy-introduces-new-karting-series/ |title=F1 Academy Introduces New Karting Series |last=Smith |first=Fred |date=13 October 2023 |magazine=[[Road & Track]] |publisher=[[Hearst Magazines]] |access-date=10 July 2025 |issn=0035-7189 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231021185331/https://www.roadandtrack.com/news/a45531451/f1-academy-introduces-new-karting-series/ |archive-date=21 October 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Austin Gale]] ({{nowrap|OK-N}}), [[James Anagnostiadis]] ({{nowrap|OKN-J}}), and [[Dan Allemann]] (60 Mini) were the inaugural winners.<ref name="AP2023"/> The five-round Euro Series was held at [[Kartodromo Internacional Lucas Guerrero|Valencia]], [[Steel Ring Třinec|Třinec]], [[Rødby Karting Ring|Rødby]], [[Cremona Circuit|Cremona]], and Franciacorta—each preceding rounds of the European and World Championships and won by [[Alex Powell]] in OK and [[Lewis Wherrell]] in {{nowrap|OK-J}}.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2022/11/17/champions-of-the-future-euro-series-2023-calendar/ |title=Champions of the Future – Euro Series: 2023 calendar |date=17 November 2022 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221207055906/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2022/11/17/champions-of-the-future-euro-series-2023-calendar/ |archive-date=7 December 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/wherrell-and-powell-win-the-title-in-franciacorta-after-an-incredible-season/ |title=Wherrell and Powell win the title in Franciacorta after an incredible season |date=18 September 2023 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024846/https://www.championskarting.com/wherrell-and-powell-win-the-title-in-franciacorta-after-an-incredible-season/ |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The Třinec round marked the first appearance of {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} competition in the Czech Republic since the 1990s.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/40951/here-are-the-new-tracks-of-the-2023-fia-karting-european-championship |title=Here are the new tracks of the 2023 FIA Karting European Championship. |last=Giuliani |first=Giuliano Ciucci |date=1 July 2022 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240713033212/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/40951/here-are-the-new-tracks-of-the-2023-fia-karting-european-championship |archive-date=13 July 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> ''TKART'' described the series that year as "a must" for the leading international drivers and teams.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://tkart.it/en/news/events/the-series-not-to-be-missed-in-2023 |title=The series not to be missed in 2023 |date=15 December 2022 |website=TKART |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250208121427/https://tkart.it/en/news/events/the-series-not-to-be-missed-in-2023 |archive-date=8 February 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Expansion into eight classes and national series (2024–present) ===
==== 2024–2025: Establishment of gearbox classes ====
[[File:FIA F2 Austria 2024 Nr. 20 Hadjar.jpg|thumb|left|[[Isack Hadjar]] served as a driver coach in the 2024 COTFA.|alt=Isack Hadjar driving the Dallara F2-2024 at the 2024 Spielberg Formula 2 round]]
With the creation of '''Champions of the Future Shifters''' in 2024, COTF expanded into all eight {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} classes: OK, {{nowrap|OK-J}}, [[KZ (karting)|KZ]], [[KZ2]], {{nowrap|[[KZ2-Masters]]}} ({{nowrap|KZ2-M}}),{{efn-num|KZ2-Masters is the over-35 [[gearbox]] class, established in 2022.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/40494/international-masters-super-cup-kz2-what-is-it |title=International Masters' Super Cup – KZ2, what is it? |last=Corradengo |first=Simone |date=22 March 2022 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=8 August 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240714134156/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/40494/international-masters-super-cup-kz2-what-is-it |archive-date=14 July 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref>}} {{nowrap|OK-N}}, {{nowrap|OKN-J}}, and 60 Mini.<ref name="S2024">{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/pressrel/champions-of-the-future-puts-shifter-categories-in-the-spotlight/ |title=Champions of the Future puts Shifter categories in the spotlight |date=29 September 2024 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710003216/https://www.kartcom.com/en/pressrel/champions-of-the-future-puts-shifter-categories-in-the-spotlight/ |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The one-round championship at Portimão was won by 2020 World Champion [[Jérémy Iglesias]] in KZ, [[Daniel Vasile]] in KZ2, and [[Anthony Abbasse]] in {{nowrap|KZ2-M}}.<ref name="S2024"/> The Euro Series was again held over five rounds at Valencia, [[Circuit du Val d'Argenton|Val d'Argenton]], [[Slovakia Ring|Slovakia]], Kristianstad, and [[PF International Kart Circuit|PF International]], being won for the second time by Turney in OK, and [[Dries Van Langendonck]] in {{nowrap|OK-J}}.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/amazing-races-to-celebrate-winners-and-champions/ |title=Amazing races to celebrate winners and champions! |date=9 September 2024 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024827/https://www.championskarting.com/amazing-races-to-celebrate-winners-and-champions/ |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The COTFA was expanded to six rounds—Cremona, Valencia, Franciacorta, [[Dubai Autodrome|Dubai]], [[Al Ain Raceway|Al Ain]], and Al Forsain—and was won by [[Luna Fluxá]] ({{nowrap|OK-N}}), defending champion Anagnostiadis ({{nowrap|OKN-J}}), and [[Priam Bruno]] (60 Mini).<ref name="AP2024">{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/44018/champions-of-the-future-academy-program-the-right-place-to-get-noticed |title=Champions of the Future Academy Program: the right place to get noticed |last=Marangon |first=Fabio |date=25 September 2024 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250701071314/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/44018/champions-of-the-future-academy-program-the-right-place-to-get-noticed |archive-date=1 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/44301/fluxa-cross-anagnostiadis-and-bruno-crowned-champions-after-a-fabulous-season |title=Fluxá Cross, Anagnostiadis and Bruno crowned Champions after a fabulous season |last=Marangon |first=Fabio |date=8 December 2024 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250115032640/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/44301/fluxa-cross-anagnostiadis-and-bruno-crowned-champions-after-a-fabulous-season |archive-date=15 January 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Fluxá became the fifth woman in history to [[List of international kart racing champions|win a major senior international karting title]],{{efn-num|[[Luna Fluxá]] became the fifth woman in history to [[List of international kart racing champions|win a major senior international karting title]], after:
* [[Susanna Raganelli]], [[Karting World Championship|World Championship]], 1966;<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://tkart.it/en/magazine/editorial/story-susanna-raganelli |url-access=subscription |title=Susanna Raganelli {{!}} Female kart driver and a World Champion! |date=8 November 2019 |website=TKART |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250424165710/https://tkart.it/en/magazine/editorial/story-susanna-raganelli |archive-date=24 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>
* [[Cathy Muller]], [[Karting European Championship|European Championship]], 100 [[Engine displacement|cc]], 1979;<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://formulascout.com/podcast-cathy-muller-on-her-landmark-f3-win-and-defying-disbelief/123250 |title=Podcast: Cathy Muller on her landmark F3 win and defying disbelief |last=Gascoigne |first=Roger |date=30 September 2024 |website=Formula Scout |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250319231148/https://formulascout.com/podcast-cathy-muller-on-her-landmark-f3-win-and-defying-disbelief/123250 |archive-date=19 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>
* [[Sophie Kumpen]], [[Andrea Margutti Trophy]], [[KF1|FA]], 1995;<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/44807/the-36th-andrea-margutti-trophy-in-lonato |title=The 36th Andrea Margutti Trophy in Lonato |date=19 March 2025 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url= |archive-date= |url-status=live}}</ref>
* [[Beitske Visser]], [[WSK Master Series]], [[KZ2]], 2010<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://formulascout.com/beitske-visser-aiming-for-w-series-title/52512 |title=Beitske Visser: The ex-Red Bull junior aiming for the W Series title |last=Wood |first=Ida |date=19 July 2019 |website=Formula Scout |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250522232318/https://formulascout.com/beitske-visser-aiming-for-w-series-title/52512 |archive-date=22 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>
[[Cyndie Allemann]] also won the [[CIK-FIA Green Helmet Trophy]] in 1999, with [[Marta García (racing driver)|Marta García]] winning the [[Karting Academy Trophy|Academy Trophy]] and [[Trofeo delle Industrie]] in 2015, all in junior classes.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.fiakarting.com/history/1999 |title=1999 {{!}} History |website=[[FIA Karting]] |publisher=[[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] |access-date=4 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250414000908/https://www.fiakarting.com/history/1999 |archive-date=14 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.autobild.es/motorsport/marta-garcia-entra-historia-karting-mundial-265679 |title=Marta García entra en la historia del karting |trans-title=Marta García enters the history of karting |last=Mancebo |first=Adrián |date=14 September 2015 |magazine=[[Auto Bild]] |publisher=[[Axel Springer SE]] |language=es |issn=0930-7095 |access-date=20 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230531193109/https://www.autobild.es/motorsport/marta-garcia-entra-historia-karting-mundial-265679 |archive-date=31 May 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/24152/puhakka-garcia-and-michelotto-win-2015-trofeo-delle-industrie |title=Puhakka, Garcia and Michelotto win the 2015 Trofeo delle Industrie |date=26 October 2015 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=20 June 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250529210312/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/24152/puhakka-garcia-and-michelotto-win-2015-trofeo-delle-industrie |archive-date=29 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>}} and the first in a [[List of kart racing championships#World championships|global championship]] since [[Susanna Raganelli]] in 1966.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.goodwood.com/grr/race/modern/the-fia-crowns-its-first-female-international-champion-since-1966/ |title=The FIA crowns its first female international champion since 1966 |last=Ostler |first=Simon |date=6 December 2024 |website=[[Goodwood Circuit|Goodwood Road & Racing]] |publisher=[[Goodwood House]] |access-date=20 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241207122018/https://www.goodwood.com/grr/race/modern/the-fia-crowns-its-first-female-international-champion-since-1966/ |archive-date=7 December 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> ''Vroomkart'' described the COTFA that year as an "innovative model based on inclusion, cost efficiency, and equal competition opportunities", noting its use of driver coaches from [[FIA Formula 2]] and F1 Academy, including [[Isack Hadjar]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/44572/champions-of-the-future-academy-program-a-new-approach-to-motorsport-training |title=Champions of the Future Academy Program: a new approach to Motorsport training |last=Marangon |first=Fabio |date=7 February 2025 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250401174012/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/44572/champions-of-the-future-academy-program-a-new-approach-to-motorsport-training |archive-date=1 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> It was used to [[scout (sport)|scout]] driver talent by the [[Mercedes Junior Team]] and the [[Sauber Academy]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://theracebox.com/champions-of-the-future-academy-2025-setting-the-stage-for-a-thrilling-new-season/ |title=Champions of The Future Academy 2025: setting the stage for a thrilling new season |last=Raspoet Adé |first=Sally |date=22 February 2025 |website=The RaceBox |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250413060931/https://theracebox.com/champions-of-the-future-academy-2025-setting-the-stage-for-a-thrilling-new-season/ |archive-date=13 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

The Shifters series expanded to two rounds in 2025 at Valencia and Franciacorta, won by the to-be World Champion in each category: [[Senna van Walstijn]] (KZ), [[Maksim Orlov (racing driver)|Maksim Orlov]] (KZ2), and [[Angelo Lombardo]] ({{nowrap|KZ2-M}});<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/45655/fierce-battles-in-shifters-at-franciacorta |title=Fierce battles in Shifters at Franciacorta |date=6 October 2025 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=25 October 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251025035050/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/45655/fierce-battles-in-shifters-at-franciacorta |archive-date=25 October 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> the former event was marred by controversy as 21 drivers, including six World Champions, [[boycott]]ed the KZ final in a dispute with the {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} over its mandating [[Dunlop Tyres|Dunlop]] tyres.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2025/05/10/kz-drivers-voice-concerns-over-dunlop-tyres-in-valencia/ |title=KZ drivers voice concerns over Dunlop tyres in Valencia |date=10 May 2025 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710003143/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2025/05/10/kz-drivers-voice-concerns-over-dunlop-tyres-in-valencia/ |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/45130/does-this-fia-kz-still-make-sense |title=Does this FIA KZ still make sense? |last=Marangon |first=Fabio |date=13 May 2025 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=2 August 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250603000529/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/45130/does-this-fia-kz-still-make-sense |archive-date=3 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> ''[[Speedcafe]]'' stated that RGMMC "[had] become the unwilling pawn in the middle of the game between the drivers and the [[FIA]]" and was unaware of the issue prior—19 of the 23 finalists withdrew or retired mid-race.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://speedcafe.com/europes-best-karters-sensationally-boycott-event-after-tyre-drama/ |title=Europe's best karters sensationally boycott event after tyre drama |date=13 May 2025 |website=[[Speedcafe]] |publisher=[[Speedcafe|BAM Media Group]] |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250615143147/https://speedcafe.com/europes-best-karters-sensationally-boycott-event-after-tyre-drama/ |archive-date=15 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/resultats/2025/cotf_1_shifters_valencia/kz_105.pdf?update=60 |title=Shifters {{!}} Round 1 {{!}} Valencia {{!}} KZ {{!}} Final |date=10 May 2025 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024827/https://www.championskarting.com/resultats/2025/cotf_1_shifters_valencia/kz_105.pdf?update=60 |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} subsequently dropped Dunlop as a tyre supplier.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/45306/fia-karting-european-championship-at-the-3rd-round-in-germany-kz-will-race-with-lecont |title=FIA Karting European Championship: at the 3rd Round in Germany, KZ will Race with LeCont tyres |last=Marangon |first=Fabio |date=25 June 2025 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=2 August 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250701073529/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/45306/fia-karting-european-championship-at-the-3rd-round-in-germany-kz-will-race-with-lecont |archive-date=1 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The five-round Euro Series was claimed by two British drivers:{{efn-num|The 2025 Euro Series was held at five venues: Campillos, Portimão, [[Leopard Circuit Viterbo|Viterbo]], Rødby, and Kristianstad.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://feederseries.net/2025/04/03/karting-in-2025-where-to-find-the-f1-stars-of-the-future |title=Karting in 2025: Where to find the F1 stars of the future |last=Albertini |first=Marco |date=3 April 2025 |website=Feeder Series |access-date=8 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250403175804/https://feederseries.net/2025/04/03/karting-in-2025-where-to-find-the-f1-stars-of-the-future |archive-date=3 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>}} reigning junior World Champion [[Kenzo Craigie]] (OK) and [[Noah Baglin]] ({{nowrap|OK-J}}), the latter overturning a 31-point deficit to [[William Calleja]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.it/news/45536/podium-and-title-for-craigie-and-baglin-in-kristianstad |title=Podium and title for Craigie and Baglin in Kristianstad |date=8 September 2025 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=8 September 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250908213557/https://www.vroomkart.it/news/45536/podium-and-title-for-craigie-and-baglin-in-kristianstad |archive-date=8 September 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Half of the {{nowrap|OK-N}} entrants in COTFA were female,<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/F1-Academy/news/the-battle-susie-wolff-refuses-to-give-up-on/10702632/ |title=The battle Susie Wolff refuses to give up on |last=Clancy |first=Rebecca |date=20 March 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250320232032/https://www.autosport.com/F1-Academy/news/the-battle-susie-wolff-refuses-to-give-up-on/10702632/ |archive-date=20 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> where [[Markas Šilkūnas]] was victorious over [[Chiara Bättig]] across six rounds at Portimão, Valencia, [[Pista Azzurra|Jesolo]], Slovakia, Al Ain, and Al Forsan; [[Conor Clancy (racing driver)|Conor Clancy]] won in {{nowrap|OKN-J}} as [[Angelina Simons]] ended third, whilst Italian prodigy [[Niccoló Perico]] dominated in 60 Mini.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/pressrel/silkunas-clancy-perico-and-godschalk-crowned-after-a-thrilling-finale-at-al-forsan/ |title=Silkunas, Clancy, Perico and Godschalk crowned after a thrilling finale at Al Forsan |date=4 December 2025 |website=Kartcom |access-date=13 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251213192240/https://www.kartcom.com/en/pressrel/silkunas-clancy-perico-and-godschalk-crowned-after-a-thrilling-finale-at-al-forsan/ |archive-date=13 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== 2026: National championships ====
In 2026, COTFA is set to expand into the United Kingdom in collaboration with [[Motorsport UK]], forming the four-round '''British Champions of the Future Academy Programme''' ('''British COTFA''').<ref name="UK">{{Cite web |url=https://www.motorsportuk.org/motorsport-uk-launches-future-academy-programme-for-2026/ |title=Motorsport UK British Champions of the Future Academy Programme revealed for 2026 |date=10 January 2025 |website=[[Motorsport UK]] |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250427201055/https://www.motorsportuk.org/web/20250427201055/https://www.motorsportuk.org/motorsport-uk-launches-future-academy-programme-for-2026/ |archive-date=27 April 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> It is also due to hold an [[United Arab Emirates|Emirati]] title,{{efn-num|'''Champions of the Future Academy Program UAE''' ('''COTFA UAE''')}} held across two rounds in November 2025 and February 2026 as another [[Glossary of motorsport terms#F|feeder series]] to the international COTFA,<ref name="F12025">{{Cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/f1-academy-expands-support-for-champions-of-the-future-academy-for-2026.eTFQ5tK0OKtu0fq22tOiM |title=F1 Academy expands support for Champions of The Future Academy for 2026 |date=24 October 2025 |website=[[Formula 1]] |publisher=[[Formula One Group]] |access-date=13 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251113134906/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/f1-academy-expands-support-for-champions-of-the-future-academy-for-2026.eTFQ5tK0OKtu0fq22tOiM |archive-date=13 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> and a single-round [[Macau|Macanese]] title at {{ill|Coloane Karting Track|zh|路環小型賽車場|lt=Coloane}} in January.{{efn-num|'''Champions of the Future Academy Program Macau''' ('''COTFA Macau''')}}<ref name="Macau">{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskartingacademy.com/macau |title=Macau |website=Champions of the Future Academy Program |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=13 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251113140619/https://www.championskartingacademy.com/macau/ |archive-date=13 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> F1 Academy are set to expand their support to nine drivers in both the British and Emirati series for a total of 27.<ref name="F12025"/> The '''Champions of the Future America Series''' ('''COTFAS''') is set to debut across 10 rounds from January to September in partnership with [[K1 Speed|K1]] at [[K1 Circuit]] in [[California]] and [[Spring Mountain Motorsports Ranch|Spring Mountain]] in [[Nevada]].<ref name="US">{{Cite magazine |url=https://racer.com/2025/12/02/rgmmc-k1-team-up-to-create-champions-of-the-future-america- |title=RGMMC, K1 team up to create 'Champions of the Future America' |date=2 December 2025 |magazine=[[Racer (magazine)|Racer]] |publisher=[[Racer (magazine)|Racer Media & Marketing Inc.]] |access-date=13 December 2025 |issn=1066-6060 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251213173051/https://racer.com/2025/12/02/rgmmc-k1-team-up-to-create-champions-of-the-future-america- |archive-date=13 December 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

== Format ==
The inaugural edition of COTF held four-day weekends: [[Glossary of motorsport terms#F|free practice]] sessions on the Thursday and Friday mornings, qualifying practice on Friday afternoon, qualifying [[Glossary of motorsport terms#H|heats]] on Saturday and Sunday mornings, and pre-finals/finals on the Sunday afternoon.<ref name="VK2020">{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/36545/champions-of-the-future-rgmmc-unveils-race-format |title=Champions of the Future – RGMMC unveils race format |last=Alosa |first=Maurizio |date=2 December 2019 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240526082252/https://www.vroomkart.com/news/36545/champions-of-the-future-rgmmc-unveils-race-format |archive-date=26 May 2024 |url-status=live}}</ref> The registration fees were {{currency|495|EUR}} per round for [[60 Mini]] and €595 for [[KF2|OK]], {{nowrap|[[OK-J]]}}, and [[KZ2]].<ref name="VK2020"/> By 2024, the round fees increased to €675 for the Euro Series and Shifters, with an additional €100 charge for free practice.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/180124_Sporting_Regulations_COTF.pdf |title=2024 Euro Series {{!}} Sporting Regulations |date=18 January 2024 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024831/https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/180124_Sporting_Regulations_COTF.pdf |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/210524_Sporting_Regulations_COTF_KZ.pdf |title=2024 Shifters {{!}} Sporting Regulations |date=21 May 2024 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024840/https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/210524_Sporting_Regulations_COTF_KZ.pdf |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> The season fees for the [[Glossary of motorsport terms#A|arrive-and-drive]] Academy Program that year were €29,000 ({{nowrap|[[OK-N]]}} and {{nowrap|[[OKN-J]]}}) and €23,000 (60 Mini),<ref name="AP2024"/> including a new [[chassis]], [[engine]]s, [[tyres]], fuel, a [[Glossary of motorsport terms#P|paddock]] area, logistical support, travel, and accommodation.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2024/10/09/the-range-of-competition-options-is-diversifying/ |title=The range of competition options is diversifying |date=9 October 2024 |website=Kartcom |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710003239/https://www.kartcom.com/en/news/2024/10/09/the-range-of-competition-options-is-diversifying/ |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Euro Series ===
As of 2025, the Euro Series is held over a three-day weekend composed of free practice (FP), time trials (TT), qualifying heats (QH), super heats (SH), and the finals:<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartxpress.com/ReadMore/results-2025-champions-of-the-future-euro-series-round-2-at-the-portimao-circuit-in-portugal |title=Results: 2025 Champions of the Future Euro Series Round 2 at the Portimao circuit in Portugal |last=Kaligis |first=Bas |date=24 April 2025 |website=KartXpress |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250518160328/https://www.kartxpress.com/ReadMore/results-2025-champions-of-the-future-euro-series-round-2-at-the-portimao-circuit-in-portugal |archive-date=18 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

* Thursday: FP + TT (morning), QH (afternoon);
* Friday: warm-up + QH;
* Saturday: warm-up + SH (morning), {{nowrap|[[OK-J]]}} and [[KF2|OK]] final (afternoon).

The Euro Series follows the general [[CIK-FIA]] points system, with a maximum of: 25 for QH, 25 for SH, and 50 for finals; [[dead heat]]s are settled via [[Glossary of motorsport terms#C|count-back]] in finals.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/070125_Sporting_Regulations_COTF.pdf |title=2025 Euro Series {{!}} Sporting Regulations |date=7 January 2025 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024842/https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/070125_Sporting_Regulations_COTF.pdf |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="FIA2025">{{Cite web |url=https://www.fiakarting.com/sites/default/files/2025-01/4.2_Prépa%20PS2025_0.pdf |title=4.2 Prescriptions Spécifiques / Specific Prescriptions |date=January 2025 |website=[[FIA Karting]] |publisher=[[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile]] |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710032426/https://www.fiakarting.com/sites/default/files/2025-01/4.2_Pr%C3%A9pa%20PS2025_0.pdf |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Shifters ===
As of 2025, Shifters is held over a three-day weekend composed of free practice (FP), qualifying practice (QP), qualifying heats (QH), super heats (SH), and the finals:<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.kartxpress.com/ReadMore/Results-Champions-of-the-Future-2025-Shifters-round-1-in-Valencia |title=Results: Champions of the Future 2025 Shifters round 1 in Valencia |last=Kaligis |first=Bas |date=8 May 2025 |website=KartXpress |access-date=9 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250617013020/https://www.kartxpress.com/ReadMore/Results-Champions-of-the-Future-2025-Shifters-round-1-in-Valencia |archive-date=17 June 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

* Thursday: FP (morning), QP (afternoon);
* Friday: warm-up + QH;
* Saturday: warm-up + SH (morning), [[KZ (karting)|KZ]] and [[KZ2]] final (afternoon).

Shifters follows the general {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} points system, with a maximum of: 25 for QH, 25 for SH, and 50 for finals; dead heats are settled via count-back in finals.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/210325_Sporting_Regulations_COTF_EURO_GEARBOX.pdf |title=2025 Shifters {{!}} Sporting Regulations |date=21 March 2025 |website=Champions of the Future |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024846/https://www.championskarting.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/210325_Sporting_Regulations_COTF_EURO_GEARBOX.pdf |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="FIA2025"/>

=== Academy Program ===
As of 2025, the Academy Program is held over a three-day weekend composed of free practice (FP), qualifying practice (QP), qualifying heats (QH), and two finals in each class:<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.vroomkart.com/news/45325/a-legendary-circuit-for-round-3-of-the-academy-program |title=A legendary circuit for Round 3 of the Academy Program |date=2 July 2025 |magazine=Vroomkart |access-date=9 July 2025 |issn=1724-9147}}</ref>

* Friday: FP;
* Saturday: FP + QP + QH (morning), {{nowrap|[[OKN-J]]}}, [[60 Mini]], and {{nowrap|[[OK-N]]}} final one (afternoon);
* Sunday: FP + QP + QH (morning), {{nowrap|OKN-J}}, 60 Mini, and {{nowrap|OK-N}} final two (afternoon).

The Academy Program follows the general {{nowrap|CIK-FIA}} points system, with a maximum of: 25 for QH and 50 for finals; dead heats are settled via count-back in finals.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.championskartingacademy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/070125_Sporting_Regulations_JR_SR_COTF_ACADEMY.pdf |title=2025 Academy Program {{!}} Sporting Regulations |date=7 January 2025 |website=Champions of the Future Academy Program |publisher=RGMMC Group |access-date=10 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250710024851/https://www.championskartingacademy.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/070125_Sporting_Regulations_JR_SR_COTF_ACADEMY.pdf |archive-date=10 July 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="FIA2025"/>

